RudeSMTP NEWS - User visible changes.
See ChangeLog for more detailed information on internal changes
See the end for copying conditions.

__________________________________________________________________________________
Release 2.1.0 - Matthew Flood 2026-08-01

	This is the release that removes the "local relay only" limitation
	2.0.0 shipped with.  It adds EHLO, AUTH PLAIN/LOGIN, implicit TLS
	(port 465) and STARTTLS (port 587) - the library can now send through
	any provider that requires encryption and authentication, which in
	2026 is essentially all of them.  Nothing from 2.0.0's API changed;
	everything below is additive.

	NEW: sayEhlo() sends EHLO instead of HELO and records what the server
	advertises.  supportsExtension() and supportsAuth() query the result.
	Required before startTLS() or authenticate() - both refuse to run
	without it, since there is nothing to check their preconditions
	against otherwise.  Falls back cleanly: a server that only understands
	HELO answers EHLO with 500/502, and sayHelo() still works exactly as
	before.

	NEW: connectSSL() opens the connection already wrapped in TLS - what
	port 465 (implicit TLS) expects - by way of rudesocket's connectSSL().
	The certificate is verified against the connected host by default;
	see setSSLVerify().

	NEW: startTLS() negotiates TLS on a connection already open (RFC
	3207) - what port 587 (STARTTLS) expects - by way of rudesocket's
	startSSL(), added in rudesocket 1.7.0 for exactly this.  Per RFC 3207
	section 4.2, a successful startTLS() discards every capability
	learned from the pre-TLS EHLO: that exchange happened before
	encryption started, so a network attacker could have altered it -
	hiding AUTH to force a weaker mechanism, for instance.  Call sayEhlo()
	again afterwards.  This is enforced structurally rather than left to
	documentation: authenticate() and supportsExtension()/supportsAuth()
	simply see nothing until the second EHLO runs.  A failed handshake
	ends the connection, matching startSSL()'s own contract.

	NEW: authenticate() logs in with AUTH PLAIN or AUTH LOGIN (RFC 4954),
	preferring PLAIN and falling through to LOGIN if a server advertises
	both but only really accepts one.  Refuses to run on an unencrypted
	connection unless allowPlaintextAuth(true) has been called - AUTH
	PLAIN and AUTH LOGIN both put the password on the wire as base64,
	which is an encoding and not encryption, so sending it in the clear
	means anyone watching the connection has it.  This is a refusal to
	send, not "ask and let the server reject it": nothing resembling AUTH
	reaches the wire when the connection is not encrypted.

	NEW: isSecure(), setSSLVerify(), allowPlaintextAuth() - see above.

	Without a build of rudesocket that has OpenSSL support, connectSSL()
	and startTLS() return false and getError() says so, matching
	rudesocket's own connectSSL()/startSSL() behaviour.

	No dependency-version change: rudesocket 1.7.1 was already the floor
	(startSSL() arrived in 1.7.0; 1.7.1 fixed a socket leak and a SIGPIPE
	crash found while testing this release).

__________________________________________________________________________________
Release 2.0.0 - Matthew Flood 2026-07-30

	NOTE: this release still speaks HELO and sends in the clear.  There is
	no authentication and no encryption, so it can only be used against a
	server that accepts unauthenticated relay - in practice a local one.
	EHLO, AUTH and TLS are the next piece of work.  The reply handling
	below had to be fixed first: EHLO is multiline by design, and the old
	code could not read a multiline reply.

	SOURCE-INCOMPATIBLE CHANGES

	- SMTP is no longer copyable.  It owns a rude::Socket and both SMTP and
	  SMTPImpl held it as a raw pointer with the default copy operations
	  intact, so any copy - passing by value, storing in a container -
	  double-freed the socket.  Copy construction and copy assignment are
	  now deleted, which turns that into a compile error.
	- version() now returns the library version, e.g. "2.0.0".  It used to
	  return the literal "rudeserver.com SMTP version 1.0", which tracked
	  nothing and named a host that no longer resolves.  Code parsing that
	  string will need to change.

	PROTOCOL FIXES

	- FIXED: a multiline reply desynchronized the session.  RFC 5321
	  section 4.2 lets a reply span several lines, each carrying the code
	  with '-' after it on every line but the last.  Only the first line was
	  read, so the remainder stayed in the buffer and was consumed as the
	  reply to the NEXT command - every later exchange reading one reply
	  further behind.  A multiline greeting alone was enough to do it.
	- FIXED: a message whose first line began with '.' lost that character.
	  Dot-stuffing (RFC 5321 section 4.5.2) doubled a '.' that followed CR,
	  LF or FF, and the first line of a message follows nothing, so the
	  receiving server stripped a dot that was never doubled.
	- FIXED: every message gained a trailing blank line.  The end-of-data
	  marker was sent as "\r\n.\r\n" whatever the message ended with, so a
	  message already ending in CRLF - which is every correctly assembled
	  message - got an extra one.
	- FIXED: "MAIL FROM: <addr>" carried a space after the colon that the
	  grammar in RFC 5321 section 4.1.1.2 does not have.  Most servers
	  tolerate it; some do not.
	- Addresses are now wrapped in angle brackets if the caller left them
	  off, as the grammar requires.  An empty sender becomes "<>", the null
	  reverse-path used when sending a bounce.

	RELIABILITY

	- NEW: getResponseCode() returns the server's three-digit reply code,
	  and getResponse() its full text.  Replies used to be judged by
	  comparing character zero to a digit, so 421 (shutting down, retry
	  later) and 550 (rejected, never retry) were indistinguishable - the
	  difference between requeueing a message and bouncing it.  Codes
	  within a class were conflated too: 250 and 251, or a 354 where a 250
	  belonged.
	- NEW: setTimeout() bounds how long to wait on a silent server; the
	  default is 30 seconds.  No timeout was ever set before, and rudesocket
	  blocks by default, so a server that accepted the connection and then
	  said nothing hung the calling thread indefinitely.  The caller could
	  not fix it: the socket is not exposed.
	- FIXED: addRecipient(), sendData() and disconnect() ignored whether
	  their write had succeeded and then waited for a reply that a broken
	  connection was never going to produce.
	- FIXED: disconnect() returned early on an unexpected reply to QUIT,
	  leaving the connection open.  The socket is now closed whatever the
	  server says.
	- disconnect() is safe to call more than once, and on an object that
	  never connected.
	- connect() rejects a null address or a non-positive port instead of
	  attempting the connection.
	- A reply that is not three digits, or a run of continuation lines with
	  no end, is now an error rather than something to keep reading.

	BUILD

	- The autotools build is gone (configure, configure.ac, Makefile.am,
	  Makefile.in, aclocal.m4, config.guess, config.sub, install-sh,
	  ltmain.sh, missing, depcomp and INSTALL), replaced by CMake.  The
	  config.guess it shipped dated from 2003 and could not configure on any
	  ARM machine - today that means every Apple Silicon Mac and every ARM
	  Linux server.
	- The dependency on rudesocket is finally declared.  Every byte on the
	  wire goes through rude::Socket, but no README, NEWS or INSTALL file
	  mentioned it, and configure.ac carried an autoscan stub -
	  AC_CHECK_LIB([rudesocket], [main]) with its "# FIXME" comment still
	  attached - that probed for a symbol a C++ library does not export.
	  CMake now finds rudesocket properly, or fetches and builds it.
	  rudesocket 1.7.1 or newer is required.
	- CI runs on Linux x86-64, Linux ARM, macOS and Windows, plus
	  ASan/UBSan, clang-format and clang-tidy.
	- Tests run against a scriptable in-process SMTP server, so the cases
	  above - multiline replies, 4xx and 5xx, a silent server, a connection
	  dropped mid-conversation - are actually exercised rather than assumed.

